-existing user LNET (\u0060LNET1-Static\u0060). The overlapping IPs are \u006010.10.35.52\u0060 and \u006010.10.35.61\u0060.\n\nWhen attempting to delete the orphaned NIC (\u0060arcvm001-NIC\u0060) \u2014 left behind after VM \u0060arcvm001\u0060 was deleted \u2014 the webhook \u0060createupdatevalidationwebhook.infrastructure.azstackhci.microsoft.com\u0060 **rejects the request** because it detects the IP conflict between the two LNETs.\n\nThe validation logic is **blocking a delete operation** due to an IP pool conflict that is unrelated to the NIC deletion itself \u2014 this is a **bug in the webhook validation logic**.\n\n---\n\n### \uD83D\uDCA5 Impact\n\n- **Scope:** Internal Dell test stamp only (\u0060choliv-test-rg\u0060, cluster \u0060ceo-clu001\u0060)\n- **No customer-facing production impact**\n- Customer is blocked from cleaning up orphaned network resources post-VM deletion\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Recommended Mitigation / Next Steps\n\n1. **Immediate workaround:** Manually delete the orphaned NIC directly via ARM API, bypassing the portal webhook (e.g., using \u0060az rest --method DELETE\u0060 or PowerShell \u0060Remove-AzResource\u0060 against the NIC resource ID).\n\n2. **Short-term fix:** The validation webhook should not apply IP-pool conflict checks during a **delete** operation on a NIC \u2014 conflict validation is only relevant for create/update. A fix should be scoped to skip IP range validation when \u0060operationType == DELETE\u0060.\n\n3. **Root cause fix:** After upgrade to 2601, the infra LNET was created with overlapping IP ranges with existing user LNETs. The upgrade process should either:\n   - Detect and avoid IP conflicts when creating the infra LNET, or\n   - Alert the operator if a conflict is found rather than silently creating an overlapping range.\n\n4. **Cluster resource:** \u0060/subscriptions/f726effd-d97f-48c1-8f47-e0bb67d2fe33/resourceGroups/choliv-test-rg/providers/microsoft.azurestackhci/clusters/ceo-clu001\u0060\n\n---\n\n